window.docContents[2].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Examples-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Example2___9___8":{"contents":"Example 2.9.8. Some examples of representations of Lie algebras are:\n\n(1) V = 0.\n\n(2) Any vector space V with \\rho = 0 (the trivial representation).\n\n(3) The adjoint representation V = \\mathfrak{g} with \\rho(a)(b) := [a, b]. That this is a representation follows from equation (2.9.1). Thus, the meaning of the Jacobi identity is that it is equivalent to the existence of the adjoint representation.\n\nIt turns out that a representation of a Lie algebra \\mathfrak{g} is the same thing as a representation of a certain associative algebra U(\\mathfrak{g}). Thus, as with quivers, we can view the theory of representations of Lie algebras as a part of the theory of representations of associative algebras.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Examples of representations of Lie algebras","header":"Examples of representations of Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Examples-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Example2___9___8"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Kostka-numbers-K____mu___lambda___/#Chapter5___Definition5___14___2":{"contents":"Definition 5.14.2. The integers K_{\\mu\\lambda} are called the Kostka numbers.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Kostka numbers K_{mu,lambda}","header":"Kostka numbers K_{mu,lambda}","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Kostka-numbers-K____mu___lambda___/#Chapter5___Definition5___14___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Chapter5___Theorem5___26___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span\u0009Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Chapter5___Theorem5___26___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Proof of Burnside's theorem\u0009Proof of Burnside's theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___3___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Section-5___6___-Representations-of-products/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.6. Representations of products\u0009Section 5.6: Representations of products","header":"Section 5.6: Representations of products","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Section-5___6___-Representations-of-products/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___6"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___4___-Reflection-functor-F_i___-/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___4":{"contents":"Definition 6.6.4. Let Q be a quiver, and let i \\in Q be a source. Let V be a representation of Q. Let \\psi be the canonical map\n\n\\psi : V_i \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j.\n\nThen we define the reflection functor\n\nF_i^- : \\operatorname{Rep} Q \\to \\operatorname{Rep} \\overline{Q}_i\n\nby the rule\n\nF_i^-(V)_k = V_k \\quad \\text{if } k \\neq i,\n\nF_i^-(V)_i = \\operatorname{Coker}(\\psi) = \\left( \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j \\right) / \\operatorname{Im} \\psi.\n\nAgain, all maps stay the same except those now pointing into i; these are replaced by the compositions of the inclusions V_k \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j with the natural map \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j / \\operatorname{Im} \\psi.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Definition 6.6.4: Reflection functor F_i^-","header":"Definition 6.6.4: Reflection functor F_i^-","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___4___-Reflection-functor-F_i___-/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___4"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext--Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Obtains an exact five-arrow sequence from a short exact complex using the displayed functors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nObtains an exact five-arrow sequence from a short exact complex and the displayed module data.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nProvides an additive equivalence from the displayed degree-zero construction to module morphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nRelates the displayed degree-zero object to an additive commutative group object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nExhibits an isomorphism between the displayed object and a left-derived functor value.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nShows that the contravariant sequence associated to a short exact complex is exact at adjacent indices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nShows that the covariant sequence associated to a short exact complex is exact at adjacent indices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nA projective resolution of the middle object obtained from resolutions of the outer objects.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe short complex of projective-resolution chain complexes is short exact.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Properties of Tor and Ext\u0009Properties of Tor and Ext\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext--Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});